rnative-id":["10.1002\/0470012285.ch2","10.1002\/0470012285"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/0470012285.ch2","archive":["Portico"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2005,2,25]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2024,9,7]],"date-time":"2024-09-07T14:27:20Z","timestamp":1725719240555},"publisher-location":"Dordrecht","reference-count":2,"publisher":"Springer Netherlands","isbn-type":[{"type":"print","value":"9789048155378"},{"type":"electronic","value":"9789401736435"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2000]]},"DOI":"10.1007\/978-94-017-3643-5_6","type":"book-chapter","created":{"date-parts":[[2013,3,10]],"date-time":"2013-03-10T20:26:08Z","timestamp":1362947168000},"page":"161-170","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Technological Aspects of Verification: Investigation of Alleged Use of Biological Weapons"],"prefix":"10.1007","author":[{"given":"Richard","family":"Guthrie","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"297","reference":[{"key":"6_CR1","unstructured":"Federation of American Scientists, Working Group on Biological Weapons Verification, Report of the Subgroup on Investigations of Alleged Use or Release of Biological or Toxin Weapons Agents, April, 1996."},{"key":"6_CR2","first-page":"18","volume-title":"Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists","author":"S Wright","year":"1997","unstructured":"Wright, S (1997) \u201cBioweapons: Cuba Case Tests Treaty\u201d, Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists, November\/December, pp18\u201319."}],"container-title":["Verification of the Biological and Toxin Weapons Convention"],"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/978-94-017-3643-5_6","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2019,5,11]],"date-time":"2019-05-11T07:36:19Z","timestamp":1557560179000},"score":34.326416,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"http:\/\/link.springer.com\/10.1007\/978-94-017-3643-5_6"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2000]]},"ISBN":["9789048155378","9789401736435"],"references-count":2,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-94-017-3643-5_6","published":{"date-parts":[[2000]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2024,10,14]],"date-time":"2024-10-14T11:40:20Z","timestamp":1728906020315},"publisher-location":"Cambridge","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Royal Society of Chemistry","isbn-type":[{"type":"print","value":"9781782626497"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2018]]},"DOI":"10.1039\/9781788010092-00495","type":"book-chapter","created":{"date-parts":[[2018,8,20]],"date-time":"2018-08-20T12:00:08Z","timestamp":1534766408000},"page":"495-514","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["CHAPTER 16. Implications for the Biological and Toxin Weapons Convention"],"prefix":"10.1039","author":[{"given":"Jez","family":"Littlewood","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"292","container-title":["Preventing Chemical Weapons"],"language":"en","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2024,10,14]],"date-time":"2024-10-14T10:32:27Z","timestamp":1728901947000},"score":33.943436,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/books.rsc.org\/books\/monograph\/1884\/chapter-abstract\/2472215\/"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2018]]},"ISBN":["9781782626497"],"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1039\/9781788010092-00495","published":{"date-parts":[[2018]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,11]],"date-time":"2026-08-11T12:36:43Z","timestamp":1786451803637,"version":"build-2736575974"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,1]],"date-time":"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1767225600000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;span&gt;\n &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Since March 2025, drone strikes by Haitian security forces and the private military company Vectus Global have killed more than 1,200 people in Port-au-Prince, according to Human Rights Watch. This paper argues that the Caribbean is no longer a bystander to remote lethal force and that the arrival of artificial intelligence in these operations, whether through facial recognition targeting or autonomous decision-making, is a matter of time rather than speculation. Mexican cartels already deploy drones against civilians and soldiers, and the scholarly literature has flagged the specific risk of autonomous weapons migrating into the hands of insurgents and armed groups across the region.&lt;\/span&gt;&lt;\/p&gt;\n &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The paper then posits a possible legal response. Haiti and Suriname are monist states, meaning ratified treaties become part of domestic law without further legislation. I test what I call the monist leapfrog hypothesis, the idea that this feature allows resource-poor states to acquire modern AI governance frameworks simply by acceding to a treaty. Drawing on comparative treaty scholarship and Haiti's own constitutional crisis, I conclude the hypothesis is limited in scope but has some value. Formal monism does not guarantee that courts apply treaties in practice, monism is not the same as self-execution, and a state without a functioning legislature cannot ratify anything at all, a problem Haiti currently faces.&lt;\/span&gt;&lt;\/p&gt;\n &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;Haiti's existing acceptance of Inter-American Court jurisdiction gives it access to binding international oversight of the drone strikes, and a declaration under Article 12(3) of the Rome Statute could open a path to the International Criminal Court without new legislation. The paper closes by proposing&amp;nbsp; dedicated Caribbean and\/or Latin American conventions regulating Artificial Intelligence and banning lethal autonomous weapons, built around regional enforcement and suggests borrowed frameworks from Europe may assist , and further argues that Trinidad and Tobago's incoming seat on the UN Security Council offers a real opportunity to advance it through diplomacy.&lt;\/span&gt;&lt;\/p&gt;\n &lt;div&gt;\n  &lt;span&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n   Ethical disclosure: AI used to format bibliography to OSCOLA standards&lt;\/span&gt;\n &lt;\/div&gt;&lt;\/span&gt;<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7260579","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,11]],"date-time":"2026-08-11T11:50:43Z","timestamp":1786449043000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Sea, Sand and Killer Robots: Towards a Caribbean Convention on AI Ethics and Lethal Autonomous Weapons Systems"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"given":"Jonathan","family":"Bhagan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"78","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,11]],"date-time":"2026-08-11T11:50:43Z","timestamp":1786449043000},"score":33.854015,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=7260579"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7260579","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"subtype":"preprint"}],"items-per-page":8,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":null}}}